detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
TypeScript is a programming language built by Microsoft that extends JavaScript. Every valid JavaScript program is also a valid TypeScript program, so TypeScript is a strict superset. The extra thing it adds is types:
let count: number = 5;
function greet(name: string): string {
  return 'Hi ' + name;
}
The : number and : string parts are type annotations that don't exist in JavaScript.

JavaScript is a dynamically typed, interpreted language that engines like V8 execute directly. TypeScript is a statically typed superset that exists only at development time. For example, this is valid TypeScript but not valid JavaScript:
interface User { id: number; name: string; }
The interface keyword and the type annotations get removed when TypeScript compiles to JavaScript.

Primitive types are the most basic building blocks of data. The core ones are:
let name: string = 'Ada';
let age: number = 36;
let active: boolean = true;
These mirror JavaScript's primitive values but are now statically tracked by the type system.

An annotation is the part after the colon that names a type:
let score: number = 0;
function greet(name: string): void { console.log(name); }
const ids: number[] = [];
The : number, : string, : void, and : number[] pieces are annotations.

Both define reusable named types:
interface User { id: number; name: string; }
type UserT = { id: number; name: string; };
These two are nearly equivalent for describing an object, but the keywords and capabilities differ.

Arrays and tuples both store ordered collections:
let scores: number[] = [90, 85, 100];
let pair: [string, number] = ['age', 30];
The array allows any number of numbers; the tuple must be exactly a string then a number.

An enum defines a named set of constants:
enum Status { Active, Inactive, Pending }
let s: Status = Status.Active;
By default the members get numeric values starting at 0, but you can assign string values too with enum Color { Red = 'RED' }.

A union combines types with |:
function printId(id: string | number) { ... }
let status: 'on' | 'off' = 'on';
The value id can be either a string or a number, and you handle both possibilities.

A type alias names a type expression:
type ID = string | number;
type Point = { x: number; y: number };
type Handler = (event: string) => void;
Now ID, Point, and Handler can be used wherever a type is expected.

Both are return types but mean different things:
function log(msg: string): void { console.log(msg); }
function fail(msg: string): never { throw new Error(msg); }
log returns nothing useful; fail never returns because it always throws.

Structural typing means compatibility is based on members, not names:
interface Point { x: number; y: number; }
const p = { x: 1, y: 2, z: 3 };
const pt: Point = p; // ok: p has x and y
p matches Point because it has the required shape, even though it was never declared as a Point.

TypeScript types live only at compile time:
interface User { id: number; }
// after compilation, this interface is gone entirely
function handle(u: User) { /* u: User only at compile time */ }
The emitted JavaScript has no trace of User.

An object type lists property names and types:
function printUser(user: { name: string; age: number }) { ... }
interface User { name: string; age: number; }
Both describe an object with a string name and a number age.

Function typing covers parameters and return:
function greet(name: string, loud?: boolean): string {
  return loud ? name.toUpperCase() : name;
}
name is required, loud is optional, and the function returns a string.

An assertion reinterprets a value's type:
const el = document.getElementById('app') as HTMLInputElement;
const data = JSON.parse(text) as User;
You're telling TypeScript to treat el as an input element and data as a User.

The primitive types are the non-object building blocks:
let a: string = 'hi';
let b: number = 1;
let c: boolean = true;
let d: bigint = 100n;
let e: symbol = Symbol('id');
plus null and undefined.
